<p class="clause" id="section-2-5"><strong>2.5 AI Output; Human Review.</strong> Client acknowledges that the Services use artificial intelligence to generate workflows, code, analyses, and other outputs (collectively, and excluding Workflow Assets (as defined in Section 4.1), “<strong>Output</strong>”), that such Output may be inaccurate, incomplete, or unsuitable for a particular purpose, and that Output is not legal, financial, or other professional advice. Client is solely responsible for reviewing and testing all Output, through qualified human review, before any production use, and for determining the Output’s suitability for Client’s purposes. Provider does not warrant the accuracy or completeness of Output and will have no liability for Client’s use of, or reliance on, Output that has not been so reviewed and tested. Unless the applicable Order Form expressly provides otherwise, Provider does not procure artificial intelligence models for use with the Services under Provider’s own account, and the Services use only Client-Provided AI Services (as defined below). The Services may use artificial intelligence models and related services procured by Provider from third parties under Provider’s own account, if and as expressly provided in the applicable Order Form (“<strong>Third-Party AI Services</strong>”) to process Client Data and generate Output. Provider will not intentionally, and will configure its use of Third-Party AI Services so that the providers thereof are not permitted to, use Client Data or Output to train or improve any artificial intelligence or machine learning model, to the extent such configuration is offered by the applicable provider. If Client connects to the Services, or directs Provider to use on Client’s behalf, any artificial intelligence model or service for which Client is the account holder or licensee (including through Client’s own API keys, subscriptions, or cloud accounts) (a “<strong>Client-Provided AI Service</strong>”), Client is solely responsible for (i) its agreement with, and compliance with the terms of, the provider of the Client-Provided AI Service; (ii) all settings and configurations of the Client-Provided AI Service, including data retention, logging, model training and data-use settings, content filtering, regional processing, and access controls; (iii) all fees charged by that provider; and (iv) that provider’s acts and omissions, including its processing of Client Data. A Client-Provided AI Service is not a Third-Party AI Service or a subprocessor of Provider, and Provider’s no-training commitment in this Section 2.5 does not apply to it. To the extent the Services generate software code or act on Client’s software repositories, environments, or other systems: (a) Client is responsible for determining and configuring the permissions and access that the Services have to such repositories, environments, and systems, and for maintaining backups of them; (b) Output may include or resemble code or other material that is subject to third-party rights, including open-source licenses, and Client is responsible for reviewing, testing, and scanning Output (including for security vulnerabilities and open-source license obligations) before deploying or distributing it; and (c) Provider is not responsible for any change to Client’s software, repositories, environments, or systems that is made by the Services within the permissions configured by Client, or that Client approves or deploys.</p>
<p class="clause" id="section-2-6"><strong>2.6 European Personal Data.</strong> Unless Provider and Client have executed a separate data processing agreement that expressly covers such data, including any standard contractual clauses or other transfer mechanism required by applicable law (a “<strong>European DPA</strong>”), Client shall not submit to the Services any information relating to an identified or identifiable individual, including any information that constitutes “personal information,” “personal data,” or a similar term under applicable data protection law (“<strong>Personal Information</strong>”) that is subject to the EU General Data Protection Regulation, the UK GDPR, or the Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection (“<strong>European Personal Data</strong>”), and Client represents that it is not established in the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom, or Switzerland unless a European DPA is in effect. Client must notify Provider before purchasing the Services if Client intends to process European Personal Data, and the Parties may enter into a European DPA through a Marketplace private offer or an Order Form signed by both Parties. Provider may suspend the Services under Section 1.4 if it reasonably believes Client has breached this Section 2.6.</p>
<p class="clause" id="section-2-7"><strong>2.7 Regulated Data.</strong> Client is solely responsible for determining whether the Services are appropriate for any data that is subject to industry- or sector-specific laws, regulations, or standards, including the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act, the Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act, and the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (“<strong>Regulated Data</strong>”), and for complying with all such laws, regulations, and standards that apply to Client, its industry, or its use of the Services. Provider is not responsible for, and makes no representation regarding, compliance with any such law, regulation, or standard, except as expressly set forth in a BAA or other written agreement executed by both Parties. Nothing in this Section 2.7 permits Client to submit any data prohibited by Section 2.1(k).</p>
<p class="clause" id="section-2-8"><strong>2.8 Agent Actions; Usage and Third-Party Costs.</strong> Client configures the workflows, policies, approval steps, permissions, and spend or usage limits under which the Services act on Client’s behalf. Client is responsible for all actions taken by the Services within those configurations, including communications sent to third parties, changes to Client’s or third parties’ systems or data, and purchases or other commitments, and for complying with all laws that apply to those actions (including laws governing electronic communications and marketing). Client is responsible for all usage of the Services under its account and for all fees charged by Client-Provided AI Services and other third-party services as a result of Client’s workflows, including usage resulting from errors, loops, or misconfiguration. Any spend, usage, or rate limits that the Services make available are tools to help Client manage its usage and are not a guarantee that usage or third-party fees will not exceed them.</p>
<p class="clause" id="section-2-9"><strong>2.9 Third-Party Integrations.</strong> The Services may connect to third-party applications and services that Client or its Users authorize, such as collaboration tools, code repositories, ticketing systems, and cloud platforms (“<strong>Third-Party Integrations</strong>”). By connecting a Third-Party Integration, Client authorizes Provider to access, retrieve, and transmit Client Data to and from it, and to act through it, as necessary to provide the Services. Client’s use of each Third-Party Integration is governed by Client’s agreement with its provider, and Provider is not responsible for any Third-Party Integration or for any act or omission of its provider. A Third-Party Integration is a Client-Directed Third Party under the DPA and is not a subprocessor of Provider.</p>
<p class="clause" id="section-2-10"><strong>2.10 Client Credentials.</strong> Client may store in the Services API keys, tokens, and other credentials that permit the Services to access Client-Provided AI Services, Third-Party Integrations, and Client’s systems (“<strong>Client Credentials</strong>”). Client Credentials are Client Data. Provider will store Client Credentials in encrypted form and use them solely to provide the Services. Client is responsible for issuing Client Credentials with the minimum permissions needed for its workflows, and for rotating or revoking them as appropriate.</p>
<p class="clause" id="section-2-11"><strong>2.11 Business Use Only.</strong> The Services are offered only for business use. Client represents that it is acquiring the Services for business purposes and not as a consumer, and that each individual who accepts this Agreement or the Terms of Use is at least eighteen (18) years of age.</p>
<h2 id="section-3">3. Confidentiality</h2>
<p class="clause" id="section-3-1"><strong>3.1 Confidentiality.</strong> Each Party (the “<strong>Receiving Party</strong>”) will take reasonable steps to protect the proprietary and confidential information and materials (the “<strong>Confidential Information</strong>”) provided by the other Party or its representatives (the “<strong>Disclosing Party</strong>”) from improper disclosure or use. The Receiving Party shall not without prior written consent of the Disclosing Party, directly or indirectly, disclose or make available, in whole or in part, the Disclosing Party’s Confidential Information, other than to the Receiving Party’s employees and contractors who need to know such Confidential Information and have executed written agreements with the Receiving Party respecting such Confidential Information in the manner set forth herein. For clarity, Confidential Information of Provider includes non-public information regarding features, functionality and performance of the Services and the Provider Platform (excluding the Open-Source Code), and Confidential Information of Client includes all data, content, files, business rules, prompts, and other information submitted to the Services by or on behalf of Client or its Users, including any Personal Information or Protected Health Information contained therein (“<strong>Client Data</strong>”).</p>
<p class="clause" id="section-3-2"><strong>3.2 Exceptions.</strong> “Confidential Information” does not include (a) information known to the Receiving Party prior to the provision of such information by the Disclosing Party, (b) information that is now or later becomes publicly known without breach of this Agreement by the Receiving Party, (c) information provided to the Receiving Party by a third party who, to the knowledge of the Receiving Party, was not bound by a duty of confidentiality to the Disclosing Party, or (d) information independently developed by the Receiving Party without any use of or reference to any Confidential Information disclosed by the Disclosing Party, provided that Client Data shall remain Client’s Confidential Information notwithstanding clauses (a) through (d); and any Personal Information or Protected Health Information included in Client Data shall additionally be subject to the Data Processing Addendum attached hereto as Exhibit B (“<strong>DPA</strong>”) or, if executed by both Parties, the Business Associate Agreement in the form attached hereto as Exhibit C (“<strong>BAA</strong>”).</p>
<p class="clause" id="section-3-3"><strong>3.3 Survival.</strong> The obligations pursuant to this Section survive the termination or expiration of this Agreement for five (5) years, except for Confidential Information that is being protected as a trade secret in which case such obligations survive for the longer of (a) five (5) years from termination or expiration of this Agreement, and (b) so long as the Confidential Information is being protected as a trade secret.</p>
<p class="clause" id="section-3-4"><strong>3.4 Compelled Disclosure.</strong> Notwithstanding anything to the contrary herein, the Receiving Party may disclose the Disclosing Party’s Confidential Information as required by judicial process or otherwise by law; provided that prior to such disclosure the Receiving Party (a) promptly notifies the Disclosing Party of any actual or threatened legal compulsion of disclosure and any actual legal obligation of disclosure, and (b) cooperates with the Disclosing Party’s reasonable, lawful efforts to resist, limit or delay disclosure.</p>
<p class="clause" id="section-3-5"><strong>3.5 Restriction.</strong> Except as expressly permitted in this Agreement or by prior written approval of the Disclosing Party, the Receiving Party may not copy or reverse engineer, decompile or disassemble any of the Disclosing Party's Confidential Information or any part thereof, or use any similar means to discover its underlying composition, structure, source code or trade secrets.</p>
<p class="clause" id="section-3-6"><strong>3.6 Workflow Assets and General Learnings.</strong> Nothing in this Section 3 limits Provider’s rights under Section 4.1 with respect to Workflow Assets or General Learnings.</p>
<p class="clause" id="section-3-7"><strong>3.7 Data Security.</strong> Provider will implement and maintain reasonable administrative, physical, and technical safeguards designed to protect the security, confidentiality, and integrity of Client Data (including Client Credentials), including encryption of Client Data in transit and at rest. Provider will notify Client without undue delay, and in any event within seventy-two (72) hours, after becoming aware of any unauthorized access to or acquisition of Client Data in Provider’s possession or control. Provider’s obligations with respect to Personal Information are further set forth in the DPA.</p>
