<h2 id="exhibit-a">Exhibit A — Service Level Agreement</h2>
<p>Provider will use commercially reasonable efforts to meet the Availability Objective described below. The Availability Objective is a target and not a guarantee, and Provider’s failure to meet it will not constitute a breach of this Agreement. No Service Credits (as defined below) or other remedies for unavailability apply unless the applicable Order Form (including a Marketplace private offer or an Order Form signed by both Parties) expressly provides for them. The “SUPPORT AND PROBLEM MANAGEMENT” provisions of this Exhibit A apply to all Clients.</p>
<h3>Availability Objective</h3>
<p>Provider will use commercially reasonable efforts to provide 99.9% Availability (as defined below) (the “<strong>Availability Objective</strong>”) for the Services within Provider’s Immediate Control. For purposes hereof,</p>
<p>“<strong>Availability</strong>” or “<strong>Available</strong>” means the Services are available for access and use from Provider’s cloud infrastructure (the “<strong>Platform</strong>”). The Services will be deemed Available if the Platform continues to operate in material accordance with the functional specifications for the Platform set forth in the applicable Documentation. As used in this Exhibit A, “Platform” means the hosting environment described above and is distinct from the “Provider Platform” defined in Section 1.1.</p>
<p>“<strong>Immediate Control</strong>” means the cloud infrastructure, configurations, and software deployed and managed by Provider within the Amazon Web Services account(s) that Provider uses to provide the Services.</p>
<p>Specifically excluded from the definition of “Immediate Control” are the following:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">a. Equipment, data, materials, software, hardware, services and/or facilities provided by or on behalf of Client’s and Client’s network services which allow the Client to access the Platform. These components are controlled by the Client and their performance or failure to perform can impair or disrupt Client’s connections to the Internet and the transmission of data.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">b. Equipment, data, advertisements, materials, software, hardware, services and/or facilities provided by third party vendors or service providers of Client.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">c. Acts or omissions of Client, its employees, contractors, agents or representatives, third party vendors or service providers of Client or anyone gaining access to Provider’s network at the request of Client.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">d. Issues arising from bugs or other problems in the software, firmware or hardware of third parties.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">e. Delays or failures due to circumstances beyond Provider’s reasonable control that could not be avoided by its exercise of due care.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">f. Any outage, network unavailability or downtime outside Provider’s Immediate Control, including any outage or degradation of Amazon Web Services or of any Third-Party AI Service, Client-Provided AI Service, or Third-Party Integration.</p>
<p><strong>Availability Calculation:</strong> Availability is based on an annual calculation. The calculation will be as follows: ((a – b) / a) x 100, where “a” is the total number of hours in a given calendar year, and “b” is the total number of hours that service is not Available in a given year. Specifically excluded from “b” in the calculation of the Availability measurement are: (1) a service interruption caused by a security threat until such time as the security threat has been eliminated; (2) reasons of a Force Majeure Event or events which are outside Provider’s Immediate Control as defined above; (3) use of unapproved or modified hardware or software by or on behalf of Client; (4) issues arising from misuse of the Platform by Client or its agents, clients or third party contractors; and/or (5) service interruption caused by Scheduled Maintenance as described below.</p>
<p><strong>Scheduled Maintenance:</strong> Provider will use commercially reasonable efforts to provide Client with at least 48 hours advance notice, unless otherwise agreed by Client, via e-mail of all scheduled maintenance activities that may result in downtime. Unless otherwise agreed by Client in advance, Provider will use commercially reasonable efforts to perform scheduled maintenance within a maintenance window from Friday through Sunday between the hours of 11:00 p.m. ET to 03:00 a.m. ET.</p>
<p><strong>Remedies:</strong> If the applicable Order Form expressly provides for credits, refunds, or other remedies for a failure to meet the Availability Objective (“<strong>Service Credits</strong>”), those Service Credits are Client’s sole and exclusive remedy, and Provider’s sole and exclusive obligation, for such failure. Otherwise, no Service Credits or other remedies will apply to any failure to meet the Availability Objective.</p>
<p>Remedies will not accrue (i.e., no Service Credits will be issued, and an outage will not be considered unavailability for purposes of this Service Level Agreement) if Client is not current in its payment obligations either when the outage occurs or when the credit would otherwise be issued. To receive Service Credits, Client must submit a written request, within five (5) days after the end of any period during which the Services were not Available that results in Availability for the then-current calendar year falling below the Availability Objective, or Client’s right to receive Service Credits with respect to such unavailability will be waived. Where a Force Majeure Event prevents full Availability for more than twenty (20) consecutive days in any six (6) month period, Client’s sole remedy is to terminate the Agreement on thirty (30) days’ written notice to Provider. In such case, neither Party will be liable for penalties or damages arising out of a failure to perform under this Exhibit A.</p>
<h3>Support And Problem Management</h3>
<p><strong>Provider Support.</strong> Provider support for the Platform can be reached as follows: support@aixle.com, and is available from Monday through Friday between the hours of 9am and 5pm Eastern Time for all support requests. Provider has structured a response plan to address such requests in an efficient and timely manner, addressing the most critical issues first. Support is provided by email unless the applicable Order Form states otherwise. Twenty-four (24) hours, seven (7) days per week support for Critical Priority requests applies only if the applicable Order Form (including a Marketplace private offer) expressly provides for it. Target response and resolution times below are measured during the applicable support hours and are targets, not guarantees. Cases will be opened upon receipt of request or identification of issue, and incidents will be routed and addressed according to the following:</p>
<div class="legal-table"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Severity Level</th><th>State Description</th><th>Target Response Time</th><th>Target Resolution Time</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1 – Critical Priority</td><td>Renders the Platform inoperative, or causes the Platform to fail catastrophically</td><td>1 hour</td><td>8 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>2 – High Priority</td><td>Affects the operation of the Platform and materially degrades Client's and/or its Users' use thereof</td><td>2 hours</td><td>12 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>3 – Medium Priority</td><td>Affects the operation of the Platform but does not materially degrade Client's and/or its Users' use thereof</td><td>24 hours</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>4 – Low Priority</td><td>Causes only a minor impact on the operation of Client and/or its Users</td><td>48 hours</td><td>—</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="exhibit-b">Exhibit B — Data Processing Addendum</h2>
<h3>Recitals</h3>
<p><strong>WHEREAS</strong>, the Client and the Provider entered into the Aixle Standard Terms and Conditions (the “<strong>Underlying Agreement</strong>”) that may require the Provider to process Personal Information provided by or collected for the Client; and</p>
<p><strong>WHEREAS</strong>, this Data Processing Addendum (the “<strong>DPA</strong>”) sets out the additional terms, requirements, and conditions on which the Provider will obtain, handle, process, disclose, transfer, or store Personal Information when providing services under the Underlying Agreement.</p>
<p><strong>NOW, THEREFORE</strong>, in consideration of the mutual covenants and agreements hereinafter set forth and for other good and valuable consideration, the receipt and sufficiency of which are hereby acknowledged, the parties hereto agree as follows:</p>
<h3 id="dpa-1">1. Definitions and Interpretation</h3>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-1-1"><strong>1.1</strong> The following definitions and rules of interpretation apply in this DPA.</p>
<p class="clause">“<strong>Authorized Persons</strong>” means the Data Privacy Contact (being the individual designated by Client as such in the Order Form or by written notice to Provider), or, if none is designated, the administrator of Client’s account for the Services, and any persons the Data Privacy Contact expressly identifies as an “Authorized Person” in writing to Provider, as updated from time to time.</p>
<p class="clause">“<strong>Business Purpose</strong>” means the services described in the Underlying Agreement or any other purpose specifically identified in Appendix A.</p>
<p class="clause">“<strong>Data Subject</strong>” means an individual who is the subject of the Personal Information and to whom or about whom the Personal Information relates or identifies, directly or indirectly.</p>
<p class="clause">“<strong>Personal Information</strong>” means any information that (a) identifies or relates to an individual who can be reasonably identified directly or indirectly from that data alone or in combination with other information in the Provider’s possession or control or that the Provider is likely to have access to, or (b) is protected as “personal data,” “personal information” or similar under applicable Privacy and Data Protection Requirements, in each case, that is provided to Provider by Client pursuant to the Underlying Agreement or this DPA.</p>
<p class="clause">“<strong>Processing, processes, or process</strong>” means any activity that involves the use of Personal Information or that the relevant Privacy and Data Protection Requirements may otherwise include in the definition of processing, processes, or process. It includes obtaining, recording, or holding the data, or carrying out any operation or set of operations on the data including, but not limited to, organizing, amending, retrieving, using, disclosing, erasing, or destroying it. Processing also includes transferring Personal Information to third parties.</p>
<p class="clause">“<strong>Privacy and Data Protection Requirements</strong>” means all applicable laws and regulations relating to the processing, protection, or privacy of the Personal Information, including all applicable U.S. federal and state privacy and data protection laws and, where applicable, the EU General Data Protection Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2016/679) and the UK GDPR (together, the “<strong>GDPR</strong>”), and including where applicable the guidance and codes of practice issued by regulatory bodies in any relevant jurisdiction.</p>
<p class="clause">“<strong>Security Breach</strong>” means any incident involving the accidental, unlawful, or unauthorized destruction, loss, alteration, disclosure of, or access to Personal Information in the Provider’s possession or control, except that a Security Breach does not include: (a) unsuccessful attempts or activities that do not compromise the security of Personal Information, including unsuccessful login attempts, pings, port scans, denial of service attacks, or similar unsuccessful attempts; (b) incidents caused by the Client, its Users, or its third-party providers (including any Client-Directed Third Party); or (c) incidents involving Personal Information that was encrypted at the time of the incident using industry-standard encryption, where the encryption key was not also compromised, to the extent and in any jurisdiction where the Privacy and Data Protection Requirements recognize an encryption exemption or safe harbor from breach notification obligations.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-1-2"><strong>1.2</strong> This DPA is subject to the terms of the Underlying Agreement and is incorporated into the Underlying Agreement. Interpretations and defined terms set forth in the Underlying Agreement apply to the interpretation of this DPA.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-1-3"><strong>1.3</strong> The Appendix forms part of this DPA and will have effect as if set out in full in the body of this DPA. Any reference to this DPA includes the Appendix.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-1-4"><strong>1.4</strong> A reference to writing or written includes faxes and email, as well as in-app actions by Authorized Persons.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-1-5"><strong>1.5</strong> In the case of conflict or ambiguity between:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(a) any provision contained in the body of this DPA and any provision contained in the Appendix A, the provision in the body of this DPA will prevail;</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(b) the terms of any accompanying invoice or other documents annexed to this DPA and any provision contained in the Appendix A, the provision contained in the Appendices will prevail; and</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(c) any of the provisions of this DPA and the provisions of the Underlying Agreement, the order of precedence set forth in Section 10.12 of the Underlying Agreement applies.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-1-6"><strong>1.6</strong> This DPA does not authorize, and Provider does not agree to process, any European Personal Data (as defined in Section 2.6 of the Underlying Agreement) unless Client and Provider have executed a European DPA (as defined in Section 2.6 of the Underlying Agreement). If a European DPA is executed, it will govern the processing of European Personal Data and will control over this DPA to the extent of any conflict. References in this DPA to the GDPR apply only to processing under a European DPA.</p>
<h3 id="dpa-2">2. Personal Information Types and Processing Purposes</h3>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-2-1"><strong>2.1</strong> The Client retains control of the Personal Information and remains responsible for its compliance obligations under the applicable Privacy and Data Protection Requirements, including providing any required notices and obtaining any required consents, and for the processing instructions it gives to the Provider.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-2-2"><strong>2.2</strong> Appendix A describes the general Personal Information categories and related types of Data Subjects the Provider may process to fulfill the Business Purposes of the Underlying Agreement. The Client discloses Personal Information to the Provider only for the limited and specified Business Purposes. This DPA does not apply to Personal Information that the Provider processes for its own account administration, authentication, security, billing, and System Data purposes, as described in the Provider’s privacy notice and permitted under the Underlying Agreement.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-2-3"><strong>2.3</strong> The Parties acknowledge and agree that (a) the Client, and not the Provider, determines the purpose and means of Processing the Personal Information, and (b) Provider acts as a Processor and not as a third party.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-2-4"><strong>2.4</strong> To the extent the GDPR applies, the Client is the controller and the Provider is the processor with respect to the Personal Information, and the Provider will Process such Personal Information in accordance with Article 28 of the GDPR.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-2-5"><strong>2.5</strong> If the Client’s use of the Services involves Protected Health Information subject to the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 and its implementing regulations (“<strong>HIPAA</strong>”), the Client shall not submit such Protected Health Information to the Services unless the Parties have executed the Business Associate Agreement attached to the Underlying Agreement as Exhibit C (the “<strong>BAA</strong>”), which will then govern the Provider’s Processing of such Protected Health Information and will control over this DPA to the extent of any conflict with respect to such Protected Health Information.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-2-6"><strong>2.6 Data Minimization.</strong> The Client shall provide, transmit, or make available to the Provider only such Personal Information as is reasonably necessary for the Services, consistent with the scope of the Services and the use of the Services permitted under the Underlying Agreement. If the Client provides Personal Information outside that scope (“<strong>Excess Data</strong>”), the Provider may, at its discretion and without liability: (a) decline to Process the Excess Data; (b) request that the Client remove or retrieve it; or (c) delete it or return it to the Client.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-2-7"><strong>2.7 Responsibility for Excess Data.</strong> Excess Data remains the Client’s data and, as between the Parties, the Client’s responsibility, whether or not the Provider identifies, detects, or objects to it at the time of receipt. The Provider’s failure to identify or reject Excess Data is not acceptance of an expanded scope of Processing and does not waive the Provider’s rights under Section 2.6. The Provider’s security, breach notification, and Data Subject cooperation obligations under this DPA continue to apply to any Excess Data actually in the Provider’s possession or control.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-2-8"><strong>2.8 Client Indemnification for Excess Data.</strong> The Client shall indemnify, defend, and hold harmless the Provider from and against any and all claims, losses, liabilities, fines, regulatory actions, and expenses (including reasonable attorneys’ fees) arising out of or relating to the Client’s provision of Excess Data, including any arising from the Provider’s Processing, storage, or handling of Excess Data before its discovery, except to the extent they result from the Provider’s failure to comply with its security, breach notification, or other obligations under this DPA with respect to Excess Data actually in its possession or control.</p>
<h3 id="dpa-3">3. Provider’s Obligations</h3>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-3-1"><strong>3.1</strong> The Provider shall only process, retain, use, or disclose the Personal Information to the extent, and in such a manner, as is necessary for the Business Purposes in accordance with the Client’s written instructions from Authorized Persons. The Underlying Agreement, the applicable Order Form, and the Client’s configuration and use of the Services constitute the Client’s complete documented instructions, and any additional or different instructions require the Provider’s written agreement. The Provider will not process, retain, use, or disclose the Personal Information for any other purpose, outside of the parties’ business relationship, or in a way that does not comply with this DPA or the Privacy and Data Protection Requirements. The Provider must promptly notify the Client if, in its opinion, the Client’s instruction would not comply with the Privacy and Data Protection Requirements. Notwithstanding anything to the contrary herein, Provider may create aggregated and/or anonymized data from the Personal Information to the extent authorized under the Underlying Agreement. Any such data derived from Personal Information must meet the requirements of Section 3.9.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-3-2"><strong>3.2</strong> The Provider must promptly comply with any Client request or instruction requiring the Provider to amend, transfer, or delete the Personal Information, or to stop, mitigate, or remedy any unauthorized processing, as required by Privacy and Data Protection Requirements.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-3-3"><strong>3.3</strong> The Provider will maintain the confidentiality of all Personal Information and will not sell it to anyone, share it for cross-context behavioral advertising (targeted advertising) with anyone, or disclose it to third parties without specific authorization from the Client or this DPA, unless required by law. If a law requires the Provider to process or disclose Personal Information, the Provider must first inform the Client of the legal requirement and give the Client an opportunity to object or challenge the requirement, unless the law prohibits such notice.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-3-4"><strong>3.4</strong> The Provider will reasonably assist the Client with meeting the Client’s compliance obligations under the Privacy and Data Protection Requirements, taking into account the nature of the Provider’s processing and the information available to the Provider.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-3-5"><strong>3.5</strong> The Provider must promptly notify the Client if the Provider determines that it can no longer meet its obligations under this DPA or the Privacy and Data Protection Requirements.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-3-6"><strong>3.6</strong> The Client acknowledges that the Provider is under no duty to investigate the completeness, accuracy, or sufficiency of any specific Client instructions or the Personal Information other than as may be required under the Privacy and Data Protection Requirements.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-3-7"><strong>3.7</strong> If the Client requests in writing that the Provider collect Personal Information directly from Data Subjects on the Client’s behalf, the Provider will only collect such Personal Information using a notice or method that the Client specifically pre-approves in writing, which contains a Client-approved data privacy notice informing the Data Subject of the Client’s identity and its appointed data protection representative, the purpose or purposes for which their Personal Information will be processed, and any other information that is required by applicable Privacy and Data Protection Requirements. The Provider may not modify or alter the notice in any way without the Client’s prior written consent. Notwithstanding the foregoing, Provider may object to the notice and Client shall indemnify Provider, in each case to the extent that the notice conflicts with this Agreement.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-3-8"><strong>3.8</strong> The Provider will not transfer Personal Information subject to the GDPR outside the European Economic Area or the United Kingdom unless it has taken the measures necessary to ensure the transfer is lawful, including, where required, entering into the applicable Standard Contractual Clauses with the Client or otherwise ensuring an adequate transfer mechanism.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-3-9"><strong>3.9 California and Other U.S. State Privacy Laws.</strong> With respect to Personal Information subject to the California Consumer Privacy Act of 2018, as amended by the California Privacy Rights Act of 2020, and its implementing regulations (together, the “<strong>CCPA</strong>”), or to any other U.S. state privacy law that recognizes a “service provider,” “processor,” or similar role, the Provider is a service provider or processor, and the Client discloses Personal Information to the Provider only for the limited and specified business purposes of providing the Services under the Underlying Agreement. The Provider will: (a) not sell or share Personal Information, as those terms are defined in the CCPA; (b) not retain, use, or disclose Personal Information for any purpose (including any commercial purpose) other than the business purposes specified in the Underlying Agreement and this DPA, or outside the direct business relationship between the Provider and the Client; (c) not combine Personal Information with personal information that it receives from or on behalf of another person or collects from its own interaction with consumers, except as permitted for a business purpose under the CCPA; (d) limit its collection, use, retention, and disclosure of Personal Information (including through its subprocessors) to what is reasonably necessary and proportionate to provide the Services; (e) comply with the CCPA and provide the same level of privacy protection as the CCPA requires; (f) notify the Client if it determines that it can no longer meet its obligations under the CCPA; and (g) engage subprocessors only under written contracts that bind them to these requirements, with notice to the Client as provided in Section 7. The Client may take reasonable and appropriate steps to ensure that the Provider uses Personal Information consistently with the Client’s obligations under the CCPA and, upon notice, to stop and remediate any unauthorized use of Personal Information. The Provider’s access to Personal Information is not part of the consideration exchanged under the Underlying Agreement. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the Provider may create and use Aggregated Data and System Data, and may use General Learnings, as permitted by Sections 4.1 and 4.3 of the Underlying Agreement, provided that any such data derived from Personal Information is “deidentified” as defined in the CCPA and the Provider (i) takes reasonable measures to ensure that it cannot be associated with a consumer or household, (ii) publicly commits to maintain and use it only in deidentified form and not to attempt to reidentify it, and (iii) contractually requires any recipient of it to comply with the same requirements. The Provider certifies that it understands and will comply with the restrictions in this Section 3.9.</p>
<h3 id="dpa-4">4. Provider’s Employees and Contractors</h3>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-4-1"><strong>4.1</strong> The Provider will limit Personal Information access to those employees and contractors who require Personal Information access to meet the Provider’s obligations under this DPA and the Underlying Agreement.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-4-2"><strong>4.2</strong> The Provider will ensure that all employees and contractors:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(a) are informed of the Personal Information’s confidential nature and use restrictions and are obliged to keep the Personal Information confidential; and</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(b) are aware both of the Provider’s duties and their personal duties and obligations under the Privacy and Data Protection Requirements and this DPA.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-4-3"><strong>4.3</strong> The Provider will take reasonable steps to ensure the reliability, integrity, and trustworthiness of all of the Provider’s employees with access to the Personal Information.</p>
<h3 id="dpa-5">5. Security</h3>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-5-1"><strong>5.1</strong> The Provider must at all times implement appropriate technical and organizational measures designed to safeguard Personal Information against unauthorized or unlawful processing, access, copying, modification, storage, reproduction, display, or distribution, and against accidental loss, destruction, unavailability, or damage.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-5-2"><strong>5.2</strong> The Provider must take reasonable precautions to preserve the integrity of any Personal Information it processes and to prevent any corruption or loss of the Personal Information, including but not limited to establishing effective back-up and data restoration procedures.</p>
<h3 id="dpa-6">6. Security Breaches and Personal Information Loss</h3>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-6-1"><strong>6.1</strong> The Provider will promptly notify the Client if any Personal Information is lost or destroyed or becomes damaged, corrupted, or unusable. The Provider will use commercially reasonable efforts to restore such Personal Information from its most recent available backup.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-6-2"><strong>6.2</strong> The Provider will within 72 hours notify the Client if Provider becomes aware of:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(a) any unauthorized or unlawful processing of the Personal Information (other than an event excluded from the definition of Security Breach); or</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(b) any Security Breach.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-6-3"><strong>6.3</strong> Promptly following any unauthorized or unlawful Personal Information processing or Security Breach, the parties will coordinate in good faith with each other to investigate the matter in an efficient manner intended to minimize material interruptions to Provider’s operations. The Provider will reasonably cooperate with the Client in the Client’s handling of the matter, including, to the extent reasonably necessary:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(a) assisting with any investigation;</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(b) providing the Client with reasonable information regarding the affected systems and operations;</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(c) facilitating interviews with the Provider’s employees and, to the extent reasonably practicable, others involved in the matter; and</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(d) making available all relevant records, logs, files, data reporting, and other materials required to comply with all Privacy and Data Protection Requirements or as otherwise reasonably required by the Client, in each case to the extent relating to the Client’s Personal Information and excluding information relating to the Provider’s other customers and information the disclosure of which would compromise the security of the Services.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-6-4"><strong>6.4</strong> The Provider will not inform any third party of a Security Breach without first obtaining the Client’s prior written consent, except when Privacy and Data Protection Requirements require it, and except that the Provider may disclose a Security Breach to its insurers, its legal and forensic advisors, and law enforcement, and, without identifying the Client, to other clients affected by the same incident.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-6-5"><strong>6.5</strong> The Provider agrees that the Client has the sole right to determine:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(a) whether to provide notice of the Security Breach to any Data Subjects, regulators, law enforcement agencies, or others, as required by law or regulation or in the Client’s discretion, including the contents and delivery method of the notice; and</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(b) whether to offer any type of remedy to affected Data Subjects, including the nature and extent of such remedy.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-6-6"><strong>6.6</strong> The Provider will cover all reasonable expenses associated with the performance of the obligations under Section 6.2 and Section 6.3, unless the matter arose from the Client’s specific instructions, negligence, willful default, or breach of this DPA, in which case the Client will cover all reasonable expenses.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-6-7"><strong>6.7</strong> The Provider will also reimburse the Client for actual reasonable expenses the Client incurs when responding to and mitigating damages, to the extent the Security Breach was directly caused by the Provider’s material breach of its obligations under this DPA, and subject to Section 9 (Limitation of Liability) of the Underlying Agreement, including all costs of notice and any remedy as set out in Section 6.5.</p>
<h3 id="dpa-7">7. Subcontractors</h3>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-7-1"><strong>7.1</strong> Provider may engage affiliates and third parties (subprocessors) to Process Personal Information, subject to written agreements imposing data protection obligations consistent with this DPA.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-7-2"><strong>7.2</strong> Provider shall remain liable for acts of subprocessors.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-7-3"><strong>7.3</strong> Provider will provide Client with its current list of subprocessors within ten (10) business days after Client’s written request. Provider will also maintain its current list of subprocessors at <a href="https://flow.aixle.com/legal/subprocessors">https://flow.aixle.com/legal/subprocessors</a>. That list identifies Provider’s cloud hosting provider and the other service providers that may receive the Client’s Personal Information from Provider. Agent harness software that runs within Provider’s hosting environment, and any artificial intelligence model or service that the Services use (including any Client-Provided AI Service), is not a subprocessor unless its vendor receives Personal Information under an account held by Provider. Provider will use commercially reasonable efforts to notify Client (including by updating the list at that address and by email to clients that subscribe to updates) of any new subprocessor at least fifteen (15) days before it begins processing Personal Information, and Client may object on reasonable data protection grounds within that period, in which case the Parties will discuss the objection in good faith and, if it is not resolved, Client may terminate the affected Order Form as its sole remedy.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-7-4"><strong>7.4 Client-Directed Third Parties.</strong> A Client-Provided AI Service (as defined in Section 2.5 of the Underlying Agreement), and any other third-party platform, infrastructure, or service that the Client requires, directs, licenses, or otherwise authorizes the Provider to use in connection with the Services (each, a “<strong>Client-Directed Third Party</strong>”), is not a subprocessor of the Provider. The Provider has no obligation to enter into a written agreement with, or obtain guarantees from, any Client-Directed Third Party and is not responsible for its acts or omissions, including any Security Breach or violation of Privacy and Data Protection Requirements attributable to it, except to the extent caused by the Provider’s own failure to comply with this DPA with respect to Personal Information in the Provider’s possession or control. The Client represents that it has independently assessed the data protection and security practices of each Client-Directed Third Party.</p>
<h3 id="dpa-8">8. Data Subject Requests, Complaints, and Third Party Rights</h3>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-8-1"><strong>8.1</strong> The Provider will promptly notify the Client, through the Data Privacy Contact or the Client’s notice address under the Underlying Agreement, if it receives any inquiry, complaint, request, or claim from a Data Subject relating to Personal Information or its Processing, and will not respond to any such request without the Client’s prior written consent, except to the extent required by the Privacy and Data Protection Requirements or necessary to confirm that the request relates to the Client.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-8-2"><strong>8.2</strong> The Provider must notify the Client without undue delay if it receives any other complaint, notice, or communication that directly or indirectly relates to the Personal Information processing or to either party’s compliance with the Privacy and Data Protection Requirements.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-8-3"><strong>8.3</strong> The Provider will reasonably cooperate with and assist the Client in responding to inquiries, complaints, requests, and claims from Data Subjects exercising their rights under the Privacy and Data Protection Requirements with respect to Personal Information, at no additional charge for requests of a type, volume, and frequency consistent with ordinary course operations. If any such request is manifestly unfounded, excessive, or repetitive, or otherwise requires extraordinary effort by the Provider, the Provider may invoice the Client its reasonable costs of compliance.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-8-4"><strong>8.4</strong> The Provider must not disclose the Personal Information to any Data Subject or to a third party unless the disclosure is either at the Client’s request or instruction, permitted by this DPA, or is otherwise required by law.</p>
<h3 id="dpa-9">9. Term and Termination</h3>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-9-1"><strong>9.1</strong> This DPA will remain in full force and effect so long as:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(a) the Underlying Agreement remains in effect; or</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(b) the Provider retains any Personal Information related to the Underlying Agreement in its possession or control (the “<strong>Term</strong>”).</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-9-2"><strong>9.2</strong> Any provision of this DPA that expressly or by implication should come into or continue in force on or after termination of the Underlying Agreement in order to protect Personal Information will remain in full force and effect.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-9-3"><strong>9.3</strong> The Provider’s failure to comply with the terms of this DPA is a material breach of the Underlying Agreement. If such failure remains uncured thirty (30) days after the Client gives the Provider written notice reasonably specifying the failure, the Client may terminate the Underlying Agreement upon written notice to the Provider without further liability or obligation; provided that such termination does not excuse payment of Fees for Services provided before the effective date of termination.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-9-4"><strong>9.4</strong> If a change in any Privacy and Data Protection Requirement or either party’s circumstances prevents a party from fulfilling all or part of its Underlying Agreement obligations, the parties will suspend the processing of Personal Information until the party’s processing complies with the requirements. If the parties are unable to bring the Personal Information processing into compliance with the Privacy and Data Protection Requirements, they may terminate the Underlying Agreement upon written notice to the other party.</p>
<h3 id="dpa-10">10. Data Return and Destruction</h3>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-10-1"><strong>10.1</strong> Subject to Section 6.4 of the Underlying Agreement, at the Client’s request, the Provider will give the Client a copy of or access to all or part of the Client’s Personal Information in Provider’s possession or control.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-10-2"><strong>10.2</strong> Following the export period described in Section 6.4 of the Underlying Agreement (or, if no export period applies, on termination or expiration of the Underlying Agreement for any reason), the Provider will securely destroy or, if directed in writing by the Client, return and not retain, all or any Personal Information related to this agreement in its possession or control, except as required by applicable law (subject to Section 10.3) and except for Personal Information contained in backups, which will be deleted in accordance with Provider’s standard backup rotation (not to exceed ninety (90) days from the date deletion is otherwise required) and protected in accordance with this DPA until deleted.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-10-3"><strong>10.3</strong> If any law, regulation, or government or regulatory body requires the Provider to retain any documents or materials that the Provider would otherwise be required to return or destroy, it will notify the Client in writing of that retention requirement, giving details of the documents or materials that it must retain, the legal basis for retention, and establishing a specific timeline for destruction once the retention requirement ends. The Provider may only use this retained Personal Information for the required retention reason or audit purposes.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-10-4"><strong>10.4</strong> The Provider will certify in writing that it has destroyed the Personal Information within thirty (30) days after it completes the destruction.</p>
<h3 id="dpa-11">11. Records</h3>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-11-1"><strong>11.1</strong> The Provider will keep detailed, accurate, and up-to-date records regarding any Processing of Personal Information it carries out for the Client, including but not limited to, the access, control, and security of the Personal Information, approved subcontractors and affiliates, the processing purposes, and any other records required by the applicable Privacy and Data Protection Requirements (the “<strong>Records</strong>”).</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-11-2"><strong>11.2</strong> The Provider will ensure that the Records are sufficient to enable the Client to verify the Provider’s compliance with its obligations under this DPA.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-11-3"><strong>11.3</strong> The Client and the Provider must review the information listed in the Appendix A to this DPA as needed to confirm its current accuracy and update it when required to reflect current practices.</p>
<h3 id="dpa-12">12. Audit</h3>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-12-1"><strong>12.1</strong> Provider maintains a SOC 2 Type II report, issued by an independent third-party auditor, covering Provider’s professional services and corporate information systems, and will obtain an updated report at least annually during the Term. As of the effective date of this DPA, the cloud environment used to provide the Services is not within the scope of Provider’s SOC 2 report. The infrastructure on which the Services run is operated by Amazon Web Services, which maintains its own SOC 1, SOC 2, ISO 27001, and other third-party attestations that Client may obtain directly from Amazon Web Services.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-12-2"><strong>12.2</strong> Upon the Client’s written request, the Provider will make its most recent SOC 2 report (and any available bridge letter) available to the Client for review. The Client will treat such report as the Provider’s confidential information pursuant to the Underlying Agreement. Client agrees that such report and information will be used to satisfy any audit or inspection right Client may have under the Privacy and Data Protection Requirements, except to the extent a supervisory authority requires otherwise or following a Security Breach affecting Client’s Personal Information, in which case any audit will be conducted at Client’s expense, on at least thirty (30) days’ prior written notice, during normal business hours, no more than once in any twelve (12) month period, and subject to reasonable confidentiality and security requirements.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-12-3"><strong>12.3</strong> The Provider will promptly address any issues, concerns, or exceptions noted in the audit report with the development and implementation of a corrective action plan by the Provider’s management.</p>
<h3 id="dpa-13">13. Warranties</h3>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-13-1"><strong>13.1</strong> The Provider warrants and represents that:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(a) it has taken reasonable steps to ensure that its employees, subcontractors, agents, and any other person or persons accessing Personal Information on its behalf are reliable and trustworthy and have received appropriate training on the Privacy and Data Protection Requirements relating to the Personal Information; and</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(b) it and anyone operating on its behalf will process the Personal Information in compliance with both the terms of this DPA and all Privacy and Data Protection Requirements applicable to Provider in its role as a processor or service provider; and</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(c) it has no reason to believe that any Privacy and Data Protection Requirements prevent it from providing any of the Underlying Agreement’s contracted services; and</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(d) considering the current technology environment and implementation costs, it will take appropriate technical and organizational measures to prevent the unauthorized or unlawful processing of Personal Information and the accidental loss or destruction of, or damage to, Personal Information, and ensure a level of security appropriate to:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub2">(i) the harm that might result from such unauthorized or unlawful processing or accidental loss, destruction, or damage;</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub2">(ii) the nature of the Personal Information protected; and</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub2">(iii) comply with all applicable Privacy and Data Protection Requirement and its information and security policies, including the security measures required in Section 5.1.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-13-2"><strong>13.2</strong> The Client warrants and represents that the Provider’s expected use of the Personal Information for the Business Purpose and as specifically instructed by the Client will comply with all Privacy and Data Protection Requirements.</p>
<h3 id="dpa-14">14. Indemnification</h3>
<p class="clause">The Parties’ liability arising out of or relating to this DPA is subject to Section 9 (Limitation of Liability) of the Underlying Agreement, and this DPA does not create any indemnification obligation of the Provider beyond those set forth in Section 8 of the Underlying Agreement. The Client’s indemnification obligations under Section 2.8 are not subject to any limitation of liability in the Underlying Agreement.</p>
<h3 id="dpa-15">15. Notice</h3>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-15-1"><strong>15.1</strong> Any notice or other communication given to a party under or in connection with this DPA must be in writing and delivered to the Data Privacy Contact identified in the Order Form or, if none is designated, to the administrator of Client’s account for the Services. Notices to the Provider under this DPA must be sent to support@aixle.com, with a copy to the Provider’s notice address under the Underlying Agreement.</p>
<p class="clause" id="dpa-15-2"><strong>15.2</strong> Section 15.1 does not apply to the service of any proceedings or other documents in any legal action or, where applicable, any arbitration or other method of dispute resolution.</p>
<h3 id="dpa-appendix-a">Appendix A</h3>
<p class="legal-note">Personal Information Processing Purposes and Details</p>
<p><strong>Business Purposes:</strong> Provide the Services to Client and create aggregated or de-identified data as permitted by Section 4 of the Underlying Agreement.</p>
<p><strong>Personal Information Categories:</strong> Business contact information (such as name, title, business email address, and telephone number); and Personal Information contained in documents, records, and data that Client chooses to submit to the Services in connection with its workflows, as further described in the applicable Order Form. Client shall not submit government identification numbers, financial account or payment card numbers, Protected Health Information, or other sensitive Personal Information unless expressly agreed in the applicable Order Form or a BAA.</p>
<p><strong>Data Subject Types:</strong> Client’s personnel and Users, and Client’s customers, vendors, and business contacts whose Personal Information is included in Client Data. Client shall not submit Personal Information of children under the age of sixteen (16).</p>
<p><strong>Processing Duration:</strong> For as long as is reasonably necessary to provide the Services.</p>
<h2 id="exhibit-c">Exhibit C — HIPAA Business Associate Agreement</h2>
<p class="legal-note">Effective only if separately executed by both parties</p>
<h3 id="baa-1">1. Preamble and Definitions</h3>
<p class="clause" id="baa-1-1"><strong>1.1</strong> Pursuant to the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996, as amended (“<strong>HIPAA</strong>”), the Client (as defined in the Underlying Agreement), which is a covered entity or a business associate of a covered entity (the “<strong>Covered Entity</strong>”) and Dualboot Partners, LLC, a North Carolina limited liability company (“<strong>Business Associate</strong>”), enter into this Business Associate Agreement (“<strong>BAA</strong>”) as of the date on which this BAA is executed by both Parties (the “<strong>Effective Date</strong>”) that addresses the HIPAA requirements with respect to “business associates,” as defined under the privacy, security, breach notification, and enforcement rules at 45 C.F.R. Part 160 and Part 164 (“<strong>HIPAA Rules</strong>”). If the Client is a business associate of a covered entity, Business Associate is the Client’s subcontractor, and references in this BAA to the obligations and rights of the Covered Entity apply to the Client in its capacity as a business associate. A reference in this BAA to a section in the HIPAA Rules means the section as in effect or as amended. Each of Business Associate and Client may individually be referred to as a “<strong>Party</strong>” and together as the “<strong>Parties</strong>.”</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-1-2"><strong>1.2</strong> This BAA is intended to ensure that Business Associate will establish and implement appropriate safeguards for the Protected Health Information (“<strong>PHI</strong>”) (as defined under the HIPAA Rules) that Business Associate may receive, create, maintain, use, or disclose in connection with the functions, activities, and services that Business Associate performs for the Covered Entity. The functions, activities, and services that Business Associate performs for Covered Entity are defined in the Aixle Standard Terms and Conditions (the “<strong>Underlying Agreement</strong>”).</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-1-3"><strong>1.3</strong> Consistent with the Health Information Technology for Economic and Clinical Health Act of 2009 (the “<strong>HITECH Act</strong>”) and the American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009 (“<strong>ARRA</strong>”), this BAA also reflects federal breach notification requirements imposed on Business Associate when “Unsecured PHI” (as defined under the HIPAA Rules) is acquired by an unauthorized party, and the expanded privacy and security provisions imposed on business associates.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-1-4"><strong>1.4</strong> Unless the context clearly indicates otherwise, the following terms in this BAA shall have the same meaning as those terms in the HIPAA Rules: Breach, Data Aggregation, Designated Record Set, disclosure, Electronic Media, Electronic Protected Health Information (ePHI), Health Care Operations, individual, Minimum Necessary, Notice of Privacy Practices, Required By Law, Secretary, Security Incident, Subcontractor, Unsecured PHI, and use. “Electronic Health Record” or “EHR” means an electronic record of health-related information on an individual that is created, gathered, managed, and consulted by authorized health care clinicians and staff.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-1-5"><strong>1.5</strong> A reference in this BAA to the Privacy Rule means the Privacy Rule, in conformity with the regulations at 45 C.F.R. Parts 160-164 (the “<strong>Privacy Rule</strong>”) as interpreted under applicable regulations and guidance of general application published by HHS, including all amendments thereto for which compliance is required, as amended by the HITECH Act, ARRA, and the HIPAA Rules.</p>
<h3 id="baa-2">2. General Obligations of Business Associate</h3>
<p class="clause" id="baa-2-1"><strong>2.1</strong> Business Associate agrees not to use or disclose PHI, other than as permitted or required by this BAA or as Required By Law. Business Associate may de-identify PHI in accordance with 45 C.F.R. § 164.514(a)-(b), and may use and disclose de-identified information for any lawful purpose, including as permitted under Sections 4.1 and 4.3 of the Underlying Agreement.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-2-2"><strong>2.2</strong> Business Associate agrees to use appropriate safeguards, and comply with Subpart C of 45 C.F.R. Part 164 with respect to ePHI, to prevent the use or disclosure of PHI other than as provided for by the BAA.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-2-3"><strong>2.3</strong> Business Associate agrees to mitigate, to the extent reasonably practicable, any materially harmful effect that is known to Business Associate as a result of a use or disclosure of PHI by Business Associate in violation of this BAA’s requirements or that would otherwise cause a Breach of Unsecured PHI. Business Associate agrees to report to Covered Entity any use or disclosure of PHI not provided for by this BAA of which it becomes aware, including any Breach of Unsecured PHI as provided below.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-2-4"><strong>2.4</strong> Business Associate agrees to the following breach notification requirements:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(a) Business Associate agrees to report to Covered Entity any Breach of Unsecured PHI not provided for by the BAA of which it becomes aware within forty-five (45) calendar days of “discovery” within the meaning of the HITECH Act. Such notice shall include the identification of each individual whose Unsecured PHI has been, or is reasonably believed by Business Associate to have been, accessed, acquired, or disclosed in connection with such Breach. Business Associate also shall provide any additional information reasonably requested by Covered Entity for purposes of investigating the Breach and any other available information that Covered Entity is required to include to the individual under 45 C.F.R. § 164.404(c) at the time of notification or promptly thereafter as information becomes available. Business Associate’s notification of a Breach of Unsecured PHI under this Section shall comply in all material respects with each applicable provision of Section 13400 of Subtitle D (Privacy) of ARRA, the HIPAA Rules, and related guidance issued by the Secretary or the delegate of the Secretary from time to time.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(b) In the event of Business Associate’s use or disclosure of Unsecured PHI in violation of HIPAA, the HITECH Act, or ARRA, Business Associate bears the burden of demonstrating that notice as required under this Section 2.4 was made, including evidence demonstrating the necessity of any delay, or that the use or disclosure did not constitute a Breach of Unsecured PHI.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-2-5"><strong>2.5</strong> Business Associate agrees, in accordance with 45 C.F.R. §§ 164.502(e)(1)(ii) and 164.308(b)(2), if applicable, to require that any Subcontractors that create, receive, maintain, or transmit PHI on behalf of the Business Associate agree to the same restrictions, conditions, and requirements that apply to the Business Associate with respect to such information. A Client-Provided AI Service or other Client-Directed Third Party (each as defined in the Underlying Agreement or the DPA) is not a Subcontractor of Business Associate, and Covered Entity is responsible for entering into any business associate agreement required with its provider.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-2-6"><strong>2.6</strong> Business Associate agrees to make available PHI in a Designated Record Set to the Covered Entity as necessary to satisfy Covered Entity’s obligations under 45 C.F.R. § 164.524.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(a) Business Associate agrees to comply with any restriction on the use or disclosure of PHI that Covered Entity has agreed to under 45 C.F.R. § 164.522 and communicated to Business Associate in writing.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(b) Business Associate will take the actions required of it under this Section with respect to access, amendment, restriction, and accounting only as directed by Covered Entity in writing, within ten (10) business days after Covered Entity’s request.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(c) Business Associate agrees that when requesting, using, or disclosing PHI in accordance with 45 C.F.R. § 164.502(b)(1) that such request, use, or disclosure shall be to the minimum extent necessary, including the use of a “limited data set” as defined in 45 C.F.R. § 164.514(e)(2), to accomplish the intended purpose of such request, use, or disclosure, as interpreted under related guidance issued by the Secretary from time to time.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-2-7"><strong>2.7</strong> Business Associate agrees to make any amendments to PHI in a Designated Record Set as directed or agreed to by the Covered Entity pursuant to 45 C.F.R. § 164.526, or to take other measures as necessary to satisfy Covered Entity’s obligations under 45 C.F.R. § 164.526.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-2-8"><strong>2.8</strong> Business Associate agrees to maintain and make available the information required to provide an accounting of disclosures to the Covered Entity as necessary to satisfy Covered Entity’s obligations under 45 C.F.R. § 164.528.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-2-9"><strong>2.9</strong> Business Associate agrees to make its internal practices, books, and records, including policies and procedures regarding PHI, relating to the use and disclosure of PHI and Breach of any Unsecured PHI received from Covered Entity, or created or received by the Business Associate on behalf of Covered Entity, available to Covered Entity (or the Secretary) for the purpose of Covered Entity or the Secretary determining compliance with the Privacy Rule.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-2-10"><strong>2.10</strong> To the extent that Business Associate is to carry out one or more of Covered Entity’s obligation(s) under Subpart E of 45 C.F.R. Part 164 (pursuant to an express written agreement between the Covered Entity and the Business Associate), Business Associate agrees to comply with the requirements of Subpart E that apply to the Covered Entity in the performance of such obligation(s).</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-2-11"><strong>2.11</strong> Business Associate agrees to account for the following disclosures:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(a) Business Associate agrees to maintain and document the Business Associate’s disclosures of PHI and Breaches of Unsecured PHI in the Business Associate’s control, and any information relating to such disclosure of PHI and Breach of Unsecured PHI in a manner as would be required for Covered Entity to respond to a request by an individual or the Secretary for an accounting of the Business Associate’s PHI disclosures and Breaches of Unsecured PHI.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(b) Business Associate agrees to provide to Covered Entity, or to an individual at Covered Entity’s request, information collected in accordance with this Section 2.11, to permit Covered Entity to respond to a request by an individual or the Secretary for an accounting of PHI disclosures and Breaches of Unsecured PHI.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-2-12"><strong>2.12</strong> Business Associate agrees to comply with the “Prohibition on Sale of Electronic Health Records or Protected Health Information,” as provided in Section 13405(d) of Subtitle D (Privacy) of ARRA, and the “Conditions on Certain Contacts as Part of Health Care Operations,” as provided in Section 13406 of Subtitle D (Privacy) of ARRA and related guidance issued by the Secretary from time to time.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-2-13"><strong>2.13</strong> Business Associate acknowledges that, effective on the Effective Date of this BAA, it shall be liable under the civil and criminal enforcement provisions set forth at 42 U.S.C. § 1320d-5 and 1320d-6, as amended, for failure to comply with any of the use and disclosure requirements of this BAA and any guidance issued by the Secretary from time to time with respect to such use and disclosure requirements.</p>
<h3 id="baa-3">3. Permitted Uses and Disclosures by Business Associate</h3>
<p class="clause" id="baa-3-1"><strong>3.1 General Uses and Disclosures.</strong> Business Associate agrees to receive, create, use, or disclose PHI only in a manner that is consistent with this BAA, the Privacy Rule, or Security Rule, and only in connection with providing services to Covered Entity; provided that the use or disclosure would not violate the Privacy Rule, including 45 C.F.R. § 164.504(e), if the use or disclosure would be done by Covered Entity.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-3-2"><strong>3.2</strong> Business Associate may use or disclose PHI as Required By Law. Business Associate may also use PHI for its proper management and administration or to carry out its legal responsibilities, and may disclose PHI for those purposes if the disclosure is Required By Law or Business Associate obtains reasonable assurances from the recipient that the PHI will be held confidentially and used or further disclosed only as Required By Law or for the purpose for which it was disclosed to the recipient, and that the recipient will notify Business Associate of any instance of which it is aware in which the confidentiality of the PHI has been breached.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-3-3"><strong>3.3</strong> Business Associate agrees to make uses and disclosures and requests for PHI consistent with Covered Entity’s Minimum Necessary policies and procedures.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-3-4"><strong>3.4</strong> Business Associate may not use or disclose PHI in a manner that would violate Subpart E of 45 C.F.R. Part 164 if done by the Covered Entity.</p>
<h3 id="baa-4">4. Obligations of Covered Entity</h3>
<p class="clause" id="baa-4-1"><strong>4.1</strong> Covered Entity shall:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(a) Provide Business Associate with the Notice of Privacy Practices that Covered Entity produces in accordance with the Privacy Rule, and any changes or limitations to such notice under 45 C.F.R. § 164.520, to the extent that such changes or limitations may affect Business Associate’s use or disclosure of PHI.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(b) Notify Business Associate of any restriction on the use or disclosure of PHI that Covered Entity has agreed to or is required to comply with under 45 C.F.R. § 164.522, to the extent that such restriction may affect Business Associate’s use or disclosure of PHI under this BAA.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(c) Notify Business Associate of any changes in or revocation of permission by an individual to use or disclose PHI, if such change or revocation may affect Business Associate’s permitted or required uses and disclosures of PHI under this BAA.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-4-2"><strong>4.2</strong> Covered Entity shall not request Business Associate to use or disclose PHI in any manner that would not be permissible under the Privacy Rule and Security Rule if done by Covered Entity, except as provided under Section 3 of this BAA.</p>
<h3 id="baa-5">5. Compliance With Security Rule</h3>
<p class="clause" id="baa-5-1"><strong>5.1</strong> Business Associate shall comply with the HIPAA “<strong>Security Rule</strong>,” which shall mean the Standards for Security of Electronic Protected Health Information at 45 C.F.R. Part 160 and Subparts A and C of Part 164, as amended by ARRA and the HITECH Act.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-5-2"><strong>5.2</strong> In accordance with the Security Rule, Business Associate agrees to:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(a) Implement the administrative safeguards set forth at 45 C.F.R. § 164.308, the physical safeguards set forth at 45 C.F.R. § 164.310, the technical safeguards set forth at 45 C.F.R. § 164.312, and the policies and procedures set forth at 45 C.F.R. § 164.316, to reasonably and appropriately protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of the ePHI that it creates, receives, maintains, or transmits on behalf of Covered Entity as required by the Security Rule. Business Associate acknowledges that, effective on the Effective Date of this BAA: (i) the foregoing safeguards, policies, and procedures requirements shall apply to Business Associate in the same manner that such requirements apply to Covered Entity; and (ii) Business Associate shall be liable under the civil and criminal enforcement provisions set forth at 42 U.S.C. § 1320d-5 and 1320d-6, as amended from time to time, for failure to comply with the safeguards, policies, and procedures requirements and any guidance issued by the Secretary from time to time with respect to such requirements;</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(b) Require that any agent, including a Subcontractor, to whom it provides such PHI agrees to implement reasonable and appropriate safeguards to protect the PHI; and</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(c) Report to the Covered Entity any Security Incident of which it becomes aware, provided that this Section constitutes notice of the ongoing existence and occurrence of unsuccessful Security Incidents (such as pings, port scans, and unsuccessful log-on attempts) that do not result in unauthorized access to PHI, for which no further notice is required.</p>
<h3 id="baa-6">6. Indemnification</h3>
<p class="clause">The Parties agree and acknowledge that except as set forth herein, the indemnification obligations contained under the Underlying Agreement shall govern each Party’s performance under this BAA. Each Party’s liability under this BAA is subject to Section 9 (Limitation of Liability) of the Underlying Agreement.</p>
<h3 id="baa-7">7. Term and Termination</h3>
<p class="clause" id="baa-7-1"><strong>7.1</strong> This BAA shall be in effect as of the Effective Date, and shall terminate on the earlier of the date that:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(a) Either Party terminates for cause as authorized under Section 7.2.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(b) All of the PHI received from Covered Entity, or created or received by Business Associate on behalf of Covered Entity, is destroyed or returned to Covered Entity. If it is not feasible to return or destroy PHI, protections are extended in accordance with Section 7.3.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-7-2"><strong>7.2</strong> Upon either Party’s knowledge of material breach by the other Party, the non-breaching Party shall provide a reasonable opportunity for the breaching Party to cure the breach or end the violation, if such breach or violation is curable. If the breaching Party does not cure the breach or end the violation within a reasonable timeframe not to exceed thirty (30) days from the notification of the breach, or if a material term of the BAA has been breached and a cure is not possible, the non-breaching Party may terminate this BAA and the Underlying Agreement, upon written notice to the other Party.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-7-3"><strong>7.3</strong> Upon termination of this BAA for any reason, the Parties agree that upon such termination, Business Associate, with respect to PHI received from Covered Entity, or created, maintained, or received by Business Associate on behalf of Covered Entity, shall:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(a) Retain only that PHI that is necessary for Business Associate to continue its proper management and administration or to carry out its legal responsibilities.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(b) Return to Covered Entity or, if agreed to by Covered Entity, destroy the remaining PHI that the Business Associate still maintains in any form.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(c) Continue to use appropriate safeguards and comply with Subpart C of 45 C.F.R. Part 164 with respect to ePHI to prevent use or disclosure of the PHI, other than as provided for in this Section 7, for as long as Business Associate retains the PHI.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(d) Not use or disclose the PHI retained by Business Associate other than for the purposes for which such PHI was retained and subject to the same conditions set out at paragraphs (b) and (c) above which applied prior to termination.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(e) Return to Covered Entity or, if agreed to by Covered Entity, destroy the PHI retained by Business Associate when it is no longer needed by Business Associate for its proper management and administration or to carry out its legal responsibilities.</p>
<h3 id="baa-8">8. Miscellaneous</h3>
<p class="clause" id="baa-8-1"><strong>8.1</strong> The Parties agree to take such action as is necessary to amend this BAA to comply with the requirements of the HIPAA, ARRA, the HITECH Act, the Consolidated Appropriations Act, 2021 (CAA-21), the HIPAA Rules, and any other applicable law.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-8-2"><strong>8.2</strong> The respective rights and obligations of Business Associate under Section 2 and Section 7 of this BAA shall survive the termination of this BAA.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-8-3"><strong>8.3</strong> This BAA shall be interpreted in the following manner:</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(a) Any ambiguity shall be resolved in favor of a meaning that permits Covered Entity to comply with the HIPAA Rules.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(b) Any inconsistency between the BAA’s provisions and the HIPAA Rules, including all amendments, as interpreted by the HHS, a court, or another regulatory agency with authority over the Parties, shall be interpreted according to the interpretation of the HHS, the court, or the regulatory agency.</p>
<p class="clause clause-sub">(c) Any provision of this BAA that differs from those required by the HIPAA Rules, but is nonetheless permitted by the HIPAA Rules, shall be adhered to as stated in this BAA.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-8-4"><strong>8.4</strong> This BAA constitutes the entire agreement between the Parties related to the subject matter of this BAA, except to the extent that the Underlying Agreement imposes more stringent requirements related to the use and protection of PHI upon Business Associate. This BAA supersedes all prior negotiations, discussions, representations, or proposals, whether oral or written. This BAA may not be modified unless done so in writing and signed by a duly authorized representative of both Parties. If any provision of this BAA, or part thereof, is found to be invalid, the remaining provisions shall remain in effect.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-8-5"><strong>8.5</strong> This BAA will be binding on the successors and assigns of the Covered Entity and the Business Associate. However, this BAA may not be assigned, in whole or in part, without the written consent of the other Party, except that Business Associate may assign this BAA without consent together with the Underlying Agreement as permitted under the Underlying Agreement. Any attempted assignment in violation of this provision shall be null and void.</p>
<p class="clause" id="baa-8-6"><strong>8.6</strong> Except to the extent preempted by federal law, this BAA shall be governed by and construed in accordance with the same internal laws as that of the Underlying Agreement.</p>
